submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': 'Lead', 
'section_type': 'intro', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The **Ring of Nestor** is a large 
Late Bronze Age gold signet ring associated with the scholarly work of Sir 
Arthur J. Evans on Aegean glyptic art and sepulchral treasures from Thisb\x00ea 
in Boeotia. In modern scholarship the name refers primarily to one 
Creto\x00d0Mycenaean gold ring, catalogued as CMS VI no. 277, whose elaborate 
engraved scene has made it a key object for the study of Minoan\x00d0Mycenaean 
religious imagery and Aegean iconography.[2][9][10] The ring has been the focus 
of sustained debate concerning its provenance and authenticity: Evans and later 
scholars discussed it alongside other unprovenanced gold rings in a sepulchral 
treasure, while subsequent authors have variously linked it to cemeteries near 
the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, to a tholos tomb at Kakovatos, to Mycenae, or to 
Archanes on Crete, and some have at times regarded it as a modern 
forgery.[3][12][13][15] Recent iconographic and technical studies argue that the
Ring of Nestor should be considered an authentic product of the first centuries 
of the Late Bronze Age (16th\x00d015th century BC), and the ring is now 
generally treated as a genuine Creto\x00d0Mycenaean gold signet despite 
lingering scepticism in parts of the literature.[2][12][19]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003044363335_2727442e', '20260628003050278271_95df3860', 
'20260628003050299549_1f273bb8'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003100861112_6bb7dd48', 
'20260628003336614886_688c3923', '20260628003434786807_5ba67672']}, {'title': 
'Identity and naming', 'section_type': 'body', 'blocks': [{'text': 'In Aegean 
scholarship the designation "Ring of Nestor" is applied to a specific gold 
signet ring that Evans made the centrepiece of his 1925 study, *The Ring of 
Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan After\x00d0world and a Sepulchral Treasure of 
Gold Signet\x00d0rings and Bead\x00d0seals from Thisb\x00ea, 
Boeotia*.[9][10][20] Evans used the Homeric name "Nestor" to evoke the legendary
king of Pylos and the broader Mycenaean world, rather than to claim a direct 
historical link to a particular individual.[10][18] Subsequent catalogues and 
bibliographic entries follow Evans in treating "The Ring of Nestor" as the title
of his study and as the conventional name of the ring at its 
centre.[9][11][21]', 'source_ids': ['20260628003050278271_95df3860', 
'20260628003050299549_1f273bb8', '20260628003336041628_4d897b96'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Modern discussions distinguish the Ring of Nestor from 
other notable Aegean gold signet rings that have sometimes been compared with it
or associated in popular accounts. A Mycenaean gold ring excavated in 1927 at 
the necropolis of Ialyssos on Rhodes and later repatriated from the Nobel 
Foundation collection is treated in news and blog coverage as a separate 
artifact and is not identified as the Ring of Nestor.[22][23] Likewise, four 
gold signet rings with ritual scenes from the grave of the so\x00d0called 
Griffin Warrior near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos are discussed as a distinct 
group, used to explore connections between Minoan and Mycenaean cultures but not
equated with Evans\x00d2 ring.[5][24][25]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003433689645_d14f4280', '20260628003433744120_f6d32c38', 
'20260628003433770983_fb326805'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The name "Ring
of Nestor" has also been applied in different ways in the literature to more 
than one object. One source describes a gold ring discovered in the 
mid\x00d01960s in tholos tomb A at Archanes, Crete, whose iconography and 
authenticity have been analysed in the context of Aegean forgeries; this ring is
treated there under the same label.[12][19] Another account refers to a famous 
ring acquired by Evans that comes "apparently" from the tholos tomb at Kakovatos
and notes that its authenticity is disputed.[15] A further discussion mentions 
the Ring of Nestor in connection with an alleged provenance at Mycenae.[13] 
These usages indicate that the term has been employed somewhat loosely for 
different unprovenanced or debated gold rings within the broader field of Aegean
glyptic studies.', 'source_ids': ['20260628003059378773_528c552c', 
'20260628003059434620_07120289', '20260628003059397820_a30abd40'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003434786807_5ba67672']}, 
{'title': 'Discovery, provenance and repository', 'section_type': 'body', 
'blocks': [{'text': 'Evans framed the Ring of Nestor within a "sepulchral 
treasure" of gold signet rings and bead seals that he associated with 
Thisb\x00ea in Boeotia, as indicated by the title of his work and the 
description in digitized editions.[10][20][21] The treasure as presented in his 
study does not derive from a single excavated tomb with secure stratigraphic 
documentation, but rather from a collection of objects grouped and interpreted 
by Evans in the early 20th century.[10][20] Subsequent scholarship has therefore
treated the ring as unprovenanced in strict archaeological terms, even when 
discussing its possible origin in relation to sites such as Kakovatos, Mycenae, 
Archanes or the area of Pylos.[1][2][12][13][15]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003050299549_1f273bb8', '20260628003335976995_37e649a0', 
'20260628003044344976_8ce7dd24'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'One modern 
overview of Mycenaean civilization notes that a famous ring acquired by Evans, 
identified as the Ring of Nestor, comes "apparently" from the tholos tomb in 
Kakovatos, and explicitly observes that its authenticity is disputed.[15] 
Another study refers to the ring\'s "alleged" provenance in Mycenae and uses 
this supposed connection to argue that its imagery should not be read as 
referring to Neopalatial Cretan rituals.[13] In a different context, a gold ring
called the Ring of Nestor is said to have been found in tholos tomb A at 
Archanes, Crete, in the mid\x00d01960s and housed in a museum there.[12] These 
statements illustrate the range of proposed or reported findspots in the 
secondary literature and underscore that no single archaeological context 
commands universal acceptance.', 'source_ids': ['20260628003059434620_07120289',
'20260628003059397820_a30abd40', '20260628003059378773_528c552c'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'In an introduction to a lecture on "Pylos and Minoan 
Crete", the Ring of Nestor is described as a gold ring dated to about 1500 BC 
and located in the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford.[8] A separate discussion of 
Evans\'s notebooks specifies that he made a drawing of the Ring of Nestor in his
Knossos notebook, now held in the Ashmolean Museum, with the entry identifiable 
by box and page references.[18] These notices, together with Evans\'s 
longstanding association with the Ashmolean, indicate that the ring central to 
his 1925 study is preserved in that museum, even though the detailed acquisition
history and archaeological context remain opaque.[8][10][18]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003050254412_80148c1b', '20260628003059498600_b0952664', 
'20260628003050299549_1f273bb8'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Popular and 
journalistic accounts of the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos have sometimes 
characterized the Ring of Nestor as the only previously known gold signet ring 
from the vicinity of the Palace of Nestor, with a newly discovered ring from 
Tholos IV near the palace described as "the only other" gold signet ring from 
the area.[3][5] However, these narratives do not supply primary excavation 
records for the Ring of Nestor itself and are part of a broader attempt to 
contextualize the Griffin Warrior finds within the history of research at 
Pylos.[3][4][5] Within specialist iconographic studies, by contrast, the ring is
more cautiously treated as unprovenanced, and its connection to Pylos is not 
asserted as a secure archaeological fact.[1][2]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003050141418_aed1ce62', '20260628003050185243_8450bd28', 
'20260628003044363335_2727442e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003336614886_688c3923', 
'20260628003434786807_5ba67672']}, {'title': 'Physical description, dating and 
manufacture', 'section_type': 'body', 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is
described in recent scholarship as a large gold signet ring belonging to the 
group of Creto\x00d0Mycenaean finger rings of the first centuries of the Late 
Bronze Age.[1][2] Vlachopoulos notes that it measures approximately 3.29 
centimetres by 2.18 centimetres, making it the fifth largest known Aegean signet
ring.[2] Event materials introducing a lecture on Pylos and Minoan Crete further
characterize it as dating around 1500 BC, a timeframe that aligns with Late 
Helladic I\x00d0II and contemporary Minoan Neopalatial or early Postpalatial 
phases.[2][8]', 'source_ids': ['20260628003044363335_2727442e', 
'20260628003050254412_80148c1b'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Stylistically,
the ring is grouped with other elaborately engraved gold rings such as the 
so\x00d0called Ring of Minos, which share technical and decorative 
features.[1][2][7] Comparative studies emphasize that the band of the Ring of 
Nestor is strikingly similar to that of the Ring of Minos, and that woody ivy 
motifs on one of the Griffin Warrior gold rings (often designated "Ring 2") 
resemble the ivy growing from the tree on the Ring of Nestor.[6][7] These 
detailed correspondences in band shape, motif rendering and engraving technique 
are used to argue that the rings emerge from the same tradition of 
high\x00d0quality Aegean goldsmithing and glyptic work in the 16th\x00d015th 
centuries BC.[2][6][7]', 'source_ids': ['20260628003050206365_86c54452', 
'20260628003050229612_88edaeb5', '20260628003044363335_2727442e'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Technical surveys of Aegean iconography and ring 
craftsmanship stress that the Ring of Nestor forms part of a corpus of unique 
signet rings whose manufacture involved sophisticated techniques and careful 
control of imagery.[2] Vlachopoulos highlights that recent discoveries of wall 
paintings, gold rings, figurines and ivories with iconographic subjects 
comparable to those on the Ring of Nestor, together with more thorough study of 
the rings\x00d2 craftsmanship, have led to a significant reassessment of their 
status, reinforcing the view that they are authentic works of the early Late 
Bronze Age rather than later imitations.[2]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003044363335_2727442e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003100861112_6bb7dd48']}, {'title': 
'Iconography and interpretations', 'section_type': 'body', 'blocks': [{'text': 
'Evans interpreted the main engraved scene on the Ring of Nestor as depicting 
what he called a "watch\x00d0dog" or "dragon" guarding an old "tree of the 
world", whose branches host what he regarded as the most populous scene in 
Aegean glyptic art.[2][19] Later analyses describe the creature in the lower 
part of the design as a "Minoan dragon" standing on grassy ground in front of 
the tree trunk, with branches spreading above.[14] The composition thus centres 
on a prominent tree flanked or guarded by a composite or mythological creature, 
with additional figures or symbols in the branches, and has been read as an 
image of a sacred tree and its guardian in a Minoan\x00d0Mycenaean religious 
setting.[14][17][19]', 'source_ids': ['20260628003059415870_549ccc1c', 
'20260628003044363335_2727442e', '20260628003059475506_8846c90a'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'In Vlachopoulos\'s reinterpretation, the key pictorial 
motif that Evans had treated as a watch\x00d0dog or dragon is identified instead
as a scorpion, a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography.[2] He
argues that this scorpion "kills" the so\x00d0called Minoan dragon of the ring 
and provides a coherent narrative reading of the scene, in which the guardian of
the sacred tree is overcome by an apotropaic agent.[2] This interpretation 
situates the ring within a wider repertoire of Aegean imagery in which dangerous
or liminal creatures are countered by protective symbols, reinforcing the idea 
that the ring\'s engraving articulates complex religious or mythological 
concepts rather than a simple decorative motif.[1][2][14]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003044363335_2727442e', '20260628003044344976_8ce7dd24'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Iconographic studies also draw attention to the presence
of incurved altars and related cult equipment on the Ring of Nestor.[1] 
Eliopoulos notes that the connection between these incurved altars and their 
depiction on the ring supports its authenticity as a piece of 
Minoan\x00d0Mycenaean religious art, since similar forms appear in securely 
provenanced Aegean contexts.[1] Comparative analyses of late Bronze Age glyptic 
motifs of a religious nature use the Ring of Nestor as a case study for how 
sacred trees, altars, guardian creatures and apotropaic symbols are combined in 
signet ring imagery to convey ritual or mythological narratives.[13][17][19]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628003044344976_8ce7dd24', 
'20260628003059397820_a30abd40'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': "The ring's 
iconography has been discussed not only for its internal narrative coherence but
also for its potential significance in broader debates about Minoan and 
Mycenaean religion. Nilsson's classic study of Minoan\x00d0Mycenaean religion 
reproduces illustrations of the Ring of Nestor alongside other key glyptic and 
fresco images and uses them to explore themes such as sacred trees, epiphanies 
and composite creatures.[17] Later authors have revisited these readings, 
sometimes questioning whether the ring's imagery should be tied specifically to 
Neopalatial Cretan rituals, especially when its supposed provenance is linked to
Mycenae or other mainland sites.[13][17]", 'source_ids': 
['20260628003059475506_8846c90a', '20260628003059397820_a30abd40'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003100861112_6bb7dd48']}, 
{'title': 'Authenticity debates', 'section_type': 'conflict_disclosure', 
'blocks': [{'text': "From the time of Evans's publication, the Ring of Nestor 
has occupied a central place in discussions of Aegean forgeries and the 
authenticity of Creto\x00d0Mycenaean gold rings.[1][2][19] Generations of 
scholars have debated the genuineness of both the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of
Minos, often drawing on stylistic, iconographic and technical arguments to 
support or question their status as Bronze Age artifacts.[1][2] Some early 
20th\x00d0century scholars regarded the Ring of Nestor as a forgery, a view that
persisted in parts of the literature and contributed to wider scepticism about 
unprovenanced gold rings.[2][12][16][19]", 'source_ids': 
['20260628003044344976_8ce7dd24', '20260628003044363335_2727442e', 
'20260628003059378773_528c552c'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later 
assessments have tended to move away from blanket scepticism. Eliopoulos argues 
that unprovenanced finger rings such as the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos
should not be disqualified a priori, emphasizing that they represent a "vast 
resource" of emerging data that can inform understanding of ancient societies 
and material culture.[1] Similarly, Vlachopoulos states that the tendency to 
treat rings with obscure or even legendary find circumstances as fakes has been 
reassessed completely in favour of their authenticity, and that his research 
confirms the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor while contrasting it with 
earlier claims of forgery.[2] He dates the ring to the 16th\x00d015th century BC
on the basis of iconographic parallels and craftsmanship, and presents it as an 
authentic Creto\x00d0Mycenaean gold signet ring.[2]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628003044344976_8ce7dd24', '20260628003044363335_2727442e'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Not all discussions accept this conclusion without 
reservation. In a study of the Isopata Ring and Aegean forgeries, Davis and 
colleagues note that, although some scholars have accepted certain debated rings
as authentic, lingering scepticism remains and that neither the Isopata Ring nor
the Ring of Nestor has achieved universal acceptance.[12] Gere\'s analysis of 
modernism and Knossos observes that the discovery of a comparably decorated ring
(sometimes referred to as Ring 2 from the Griffin Warrior tomb) has been 
presented as evidence that "the so\x00d0called Ring of Nestor" is authentic and 
not the fake it had often been considered, implicitly acknowledging the prior 
prevalence of doubts.[16][6] These comments show that, while recent technical 
and iconographic work has strengthened the case for authenticity, the ring\'s 
status continues to be discussed critically in the scholarly literature.', 
'source_ids': ['20260628003059378773_528c552c', '20260628003059452087_6d8f4805',
'20260628003050206365_86c54452'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}], 
'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003100861112_6bb7dd48']}, {'title': 
'Reception and legacy', 'section_type': 'body', 'blocks': [{'text': 'Because of 
its size, elaborate iconography and contested authenticity, the Ring of Nestor 
has played an outsized role in modern interpretations of Aegean art and 
religion. Evans used it as a key illustration in arguing for a rich Minoan 
afterlife belief and for complex mythological narratives in glyptic scenes, 
influencing early 20th\x00d0century views of Minoan religion.[10][18][17] 
Nilsson and other foundational scholars incorporated the ring into syntheses of 
Minoan\x00d0Mycenaean religion, reinforcing its status as a standard reference 
image for sacred trees, guardian creatures and epiphany scenes.[17][19]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628003059475506_8846c90a', 
'20260628003059498600_b0952664'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'In later 
scholarship the ring has become a touchstone in debates about the reliability of
unprovenanced artifacts and the dangers of forgeries in reconstructing ancient 
religious systems.[1][2][12][19] Discussions of Aegean forgeries frequently 
mention the Ring of Nestor alongside the Ring of Minos and the Isopata Ring as 
emblematic cases in which questions of authenticity, provenance and modern 
reception intersect.[12][16][19] At the same time, technical and iconographic 
analyses that argue for its authenticity have led to its renewed use as 
comparative material in studies of newly excavated rings, such as those from the
Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos, and in broader examinations of early Late Bronze 
Age craftsmanship.[2][5][6][7]', 'source_ids': ['20260628003044363335_2727442e',
'20260628003059378773_528c552c', '20260628003050185243_8450bd28'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260628003100861112_6bb7dd48']}]})
