───────────────────────────────── Final Report ─────────────────────────────────
                                 Ring of Nestor                                 

The Ring of Nestor is a large Late Bronze Age gold signet ring associated with  
the scholarly work of Sir Arthur J. Evans on Aegean glyptic art and sepulchral  
treasures from Thisb�ea in Boeotia. In modern scholarship the name refers       
primarily to one Creto�d0Mycenaean gold ring, catalogued as CMS VI no. 277,     
whose elaborate engraved scene has made it a key object for the study of        
Minoan�d0Mycenaean religious imagery and Aegean iconography. The ring has been  
the focus of sustained debate concerning its provenance and authenticity: Evans 
and later scholars discussed it alongside other unprovenanced gold rings in a   
sepulchral treasure, while subsequent authors have variously linked it to       
cemeteries near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, to a tholos tomb at Kakovatos, to
Mycenae, or to Archanes on Crete, and some have at times regarded it as a modern
forgery. Recent iconographic and technical studies argue that the Ring of Nestor
should be considered an authentic product of the first centuries of the Late    
Bronze Age (16th�d015th century BC), and the ring is now generally treated as a 
genuine Creto�d0Mycenaean gold signet despite lingering scepticism in parts of  
the literature. [1]                                                             

Identity and naming                                                             

In Aegean scholarship the designation "Ring of Nestor" is applied to a specific 
gold signet ring that Evans made the centrepiece of his 1925 study, The Ring of 
Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan After�d0world and a Sepulchral Treasure of    
Gold Signet�d0rings and Bead�d0seals from Thisb�ea, Boeotia. Evans used the     
Homeric name "Nestor" to evoke the legendary king of Pylos and the broader      
Mycenaean world, rather than to claim a direct historical link to a particular  
individual. Subsequent catalogues and bibliographic entries follow Evans in     
treating "The Ring of Nestor" as the title of his study and as the conventional 
name of the ring at its centre. [2][3]                                          

The name "Ring of Nestor" has also been applied in different ways in the        
literature to more than one object. One source describes a gold ring discovered 
in the mid�d01960s in tholos tomb A at Archanes, Crete, whose iconography and   
authenticity have been analysed in the context of Aegean forgeries; this ring is
treated there under the same label. Another account refers to a famous ring     
acquired by Evans that comes "apparently" from the tholos tomb at Kakovatos and 
notes that its authenticity is disputed. A further discussion mentions the Ring 
of Nestor in connection with an alleged provenance at Mycenae. These usages     
indicate that the term has been employed somewhat loosely for different         
unprovenanced or debated gold rings within the broader field of Aegean glyptic  
studies. [1]                                                                    

Discovery, provenance and repository                                            

Evans framed the Ring of Nestor within a "sepulchral treasure" of gold signet   
rings and bead seals that he associated with Thisb�ea in Boeotia, as indicated  
by the title of his work and the description in digitized editions. The treasure
as presented in his study does not derive from a single excavated tomb with     
secure stratigraphic documentation, but rather from a collection of objects     
grouped and interpreted by Evans in the early 20th century. Subsequent          
scholarship has therefore treated the ring as unprovenanced in strict           
archaeological terms, even when discussing its possible origin in relation to   
sites such as Kakovatos, Mycenae, Archanes or the area of Pylos. [3][4]         

One modern overview of Mycenaean civilization notes that a famous ring acquired 
by Evans, identified as the Ring of Nestor, comes "apparently" from the tholos  
tomb in Kakovatos, and explicitly observes that its authenticity is disputed.   
Another study refers to the ring's "alleged" provenance in Mycenae and uses this
supposed connection to argue that its imagery should not be read as referring to
Neopalatial Cretan rituals. In a different context, a gold ring called the Ring 
of Nestor is said to have been found in tholos tomb A at Archanes, Crete, in the
mid�d01960s and housed in a museum there. These statements illustrate the range 
of proposed or reported findspots in the secondary literature and underscore    
that no single archaeological context commands universal acceptance. [1]        

In an introduction to a lecture on "Pylos and Minoan Crete", the Ring of Nestor 
is described as a gold ring dated to about 1500 BC and located in the Ashmolean 
Museum in Oxford. A separate discussion of Evans's notebooks specifies that he  
made a drawing of the Ring of Nestor in his Knossos notebook, now held in the   
Ashmolean Museum, with the entry identifiable by box and page references. These 
notices, together with Evans's longstanding association with the Ashmolean,     
indicate that the ring central to his 1925 study is preserved in that museum,   
even though the detailed acquisition history and archaeological context remain  
opaque. [3]                                                                     

Popular and journalistic accounts of the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos have     
sometimes characterized the Ring of Nestor as the only previously known gold    
signet ring from the vicinity of the Palace of Nestor, with a newly discovered  
ring from Tholos IV near the palace described as "the only other" gold signet   
ring from the area. However, these narratives do not supply primary excavation  
records for the Ring of Nestor itself and are part of a broader attempt to      
contextualize the Griffin Warrior finds within the history of research at Pylos.
Within specialist iconographic studies, by contrast, the ring is more cautiously
treated as unprovenanced, and its connection to Pylos is not asserted as a      
secure archaeological fact. [5]                                                 

Physical description, dating and manufacture                                    

The Ring of Nestor is described in recent scholarship as a large gold signet    
ring belonging to the group of Creto�d0Mycenaean finger rings of the first      
centuries of the Late Bronze Age. Vlachopoulos notes that it measures           
approximately 3.29 centimetres by 2.18 centimetres, making it the fifth largest 
known Aegean signet ring. Event materials introducing a lecture on Pylos and    
Minoan Crete further characterize it as dating around 1500 BC, a timeframe that 
aligns with Late Helladic I�d0II and contemporary Minoan Neopalatial or early   
Postpalatial phases. [5]                                                        

Stylistically, the ring is grouped with other elaborately engraved gold rings   
such as the so�d0called Ring of Minos, which share technical and decorative     
features. Comparative studies emphasize that the band of the Ring of Nestor is  
strikingly similar to that of the Ring of Minos, and that woody ivy motifs on   
one of the Griffin Warrior gold rings (often designated "Ring 2") resemble the  
ivy growing from the tree on the Ring of Nestor. These detailed correspondences 
in band shape, motif rendering and engraving technique are used to argue that   
the rings emerge from the same tradition of high�d0quality Aegean goldsmithing  
and glyptic work in the 16th�d015th centuries BC. [5]                           

Technical surveys of Aegean iconography and ring craftsmanship stress that the  
Ring of Nestor forms part of a corpus of unique signet rings whose manufacture  
involved sophisticated techniques and careful control of imagery. Vlachopoulos  
highlights that recent discoveries of wall paintings, gold rings, figurines and 
ivories with iconographic subjects comparable to those on the Ring of Nestor,   
together with more thorough study of the rings�d2 craftsmanship, have led to a  
significant reassessment of their status, reinforcing the view that they are    
authentic works of the early Late Bronze Age rather than later imitations. [5]  

Iconography and interpretations                                                 

Evans interpreted the main engraved scene on the Ring of Nestor as depicting    
what he called a "watch�d0dog" or "dragon" guarding an old "tree of the world", 
whose branches host what he regarded as the most populous scene in Aegean       
glyptic art. Later analyses describe the creature in the lower part of the      
design as a "Minoan dragon" standing on grassy ground in front of the tree      
trunk, with branches spreading above. The composition thus centres on a         
prominent tree flanked or guarded by a composite or mythological creature, with 
additional figures or symbols in the branches, and has been read as an image of 
a sacred tree and its guardian in a Minoan�d0Mycenaean religious setting. [6]   

In Vlachopoulos's reinterpretation, the key pictorial motif that Evans had      
treated as a watch�d0dog or dragon is identified instead as a scorpion, a major 
apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography. He argues that this scorpion
"kills" the so�d0called Minoan dragon of the ring and provides a coherent       
narrative reading of the scene, in which the guardian of the sacred tree is     
overcome by an apotropaic agent. This interpretation situates the ring within a 
wider repertoire of Aegean imagery in which dangerous or liminal creatures are  
countered by protective symbols, reinforcing the idea that the ring's engraving 
articulates complex religious or mythological concepts rather than a simple     
decorative motif. [7][5]                                                        

Iconographic studies also draw attention to the presence of incurved altars and 
related cult equipment on the Ring of Nestor. Eliopoulos notes that the         
connection between these incurved altars and their depiction on the ring        
supports its authenticity as a piece of Minoan�d0Mycenaean religious art, since 
similar forms appear in securely provenanced Aegean contexts. Comparative       
analyses of late Bronze Age glyptic motifs of a religious nature use the Ring of
Nestor as a case study for how sacred trees, altars, guardian creatures and     
apotropaic symbols are combined in signet ring imagery to convey ritual or      
mythological narratives. [8]                                                    

The ring's iconography has been discussed not only for its internal narrative   
coherence but also for its potential significance in broader debates about      
Minoan and Mycenaean religion. Nilsson's classic study of Minoan�d0Mycenaean    
religion reproduces illustrations of the Ring of Nestor alongside other key     
glyptic and fresco images and uses them to explore themes such as sacred trees, 
epiphanies and composite creatures. Later authors have revisited these readings,
sometimes questioning whether the ring's imagery should be tied specifically to 
Neopalatial Cretan rituals, especially when its supposed provenance is linked to
Mycenae or other mainland sites. [8]                                            

Authenticity debates                                                            

From the time of Evans's publication, the Ring of Nestor has occupied a central 
place in discussions of Aegean forgeries and the authenticity of                
Creto�d0Mycenaean gold rings. Generations of scholars have debated the          
genuineness of both the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos, often drawing on  
stylistic, iconographic and technical arguments to support or question their    
status as Bronze Age artifacts. Some early 20th�d0century scholars regarded the 
Ring of Nestor as a forgery, a view that persisted in parts of the literature   
and contributed to wider scepticism about unprovenanced gold rings. [1]         

Later assessments have tended to move away from blanket scepticism. Eliopoulos  
argues that unprovenanced finger rings such as the Ring of Nestor and the Ring  
of Minos should not be disqualified a priori, emphasizing that they represent a 
"vast resource" of emerging data that can inform understanding of ancient       
societies and material culture. Similarly, Vlachopoulos states that the tendency
to treat rings with obscure or even legendary find circumstances as fakes has   
been reassessed completely in favour of their authenticity, and that his        
research confirms the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor while contrasting it   
with earlier claims of forgery. He dates the ring to the 16th�d015th century BC 
on the basis of iconographic parallels and craftsmanship, and presents it as an 
authentic Creto�d0Mycenaean gold signet ring. [7][5]                            

Not all discussions accept this conclusion without reservation. In a study of   
the Isopata Ring and Aegean forgeries, Davis and colleagues note that, although 
some scholars have accepted certain debated rings as authentic, lingering       
scepticism remains and that neither the Isopata Ring nor the Ring of Nestor has 
achieved universal acceptance. Gere's analysis of modernism and Knossos observes
that the discovery of a comparably decorated ring (sometimes referred to as Ring
2 from the Griffin Warrior tomb) has been presented as evidence that "the       
so�d0called Ring of Nestor" is authentic and not the fake it had often been     
considered, implicitly acknowledging the prior prevalence of doubts. These      
comments show that, while recent technical and iconographic work has            
strengthened the case for authenticity, the ring's status continues to be       
discussed critically in the scholarly literature. [1]                           

Reception and legacy                                                            

Because of its size, elaborate iconography and contested authenticity, the Ring 
of Nestor has played a notable role in modern interpretations of Aegean art and 
religion. Evans used it as a key illustration in arguing for a rich Minoan      
afterlife belief and for complex mythological narratives in glyptic scenes,     
influencing early 20th�d0century views of Minoan religion. Nilsson and other    
foundational scholars incorporated the ring into syntheses of Minoan�d0Mycenaean
religion, reinforcing its status as a standard reference image for sacred trees,
guardian creatures and epiphany scenes. [9]                                     

In later scholarship the ring has become a touchstone in debates about the      
reliability of unprovenanced artifacts and the dangers of forgeries in          
reconstructing ancient religious systems. Discussions of Aegean forgeries       
frequently mention the Ring of Nestor alongside the Ring of Minos and the       
Isopata Ring as emblematic cases in which questions of authenticity, provenance 
and modern reception intersect. At the same time, technical and iconographic    
analyses that argue for its authenticity have led to its renewed use as         
comparative material in studies of newly excavated rings, such as those from the
Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos, and in broader examinations of early Late Bronze 
Age craftsmanship. [1]                                                          

